n \\(y=c\\);\n* parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis: equation \\(x=c\\);\n* parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\): equation \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsider a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis* (\\(y=c\\)), then any point \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) satisfies \\(b=c\\). Consequently all such points have the same value of \\(a+b\\); i.e. they all belong to a single diagonal level \\(D_{a+c}\\). Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets **at most one** diagonal level.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis* (\\(x=c\\)), the same argument shows that all points of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) lie on a single diagonal level.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)* (\\(x+y=c\\)), then every point of \\(\\ell\\) already satisfies \\(a+b=c\\); thus \\(\\ell\\) is itself a diagonal level (or a translate thereof). Since the only diagonal levels that intersect \\(S_n\\) are those with \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), such a line coincides with exactly one of the sets \\(D_t\\). Hence again \\(\\ell\\) meets **exactly one** diagonal level.\n\nTherefore:\n\n> **Claim 1.** A non\u2011sunny line can intersect at most one diagonal level \\(D_t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Behaviour of sunny lines\n\nA **sunny** line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions. Hence its slope is different from \\(0\\), from \\(\\infty\\), and from \\(-1\\). Consequently the function \\(a+b\\) is not constant along the line, and the line is not parallel to any line \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line. The restriction of the linear map \\((x,y)\\mapsto x+y\\) to \\(\\ell\\) is a non\u2011constant affine function; therefore its image is an interval of real numbers. Since the diagonal levels correspond to integer values of \\(x+y\\), the line can intersect at most **two** consecutive diagonal levels. Indeed, if \\(\\ell\\) met three distinct levels \\(D_{t_1},D_{t_2},D_{t_3}\\) with \\(t_1<t_2<t_3\\