els (there are none) would be covered by non\u2011sunny lines; the count of non\u2011sunny lines is \\(n-k\\). All lines are distinct by construction.\n\n   In both parity cases the construction yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, all distinct, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them. Hence every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1) is attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the necessary condition (1) with the explicit constructions, we obtain the complete description of admissible \\(k\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil,\\;\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\right\\rceil+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) form a partition of \\(S_n\\); there are exactly \\(n\\) of them.  \nWe call a line **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions\n\\[\n\\text{(i)}\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad\n\\text{(ii)}\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad\n\\text{(iii)}\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (non\u2011sunny lines)\n\nA line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions can intersect at most one diagonal level \\(D_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is \\(y=c\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) then every point on it has the same \\(y\\)-coordinate, hence the same value of \\(a+b\\); consequently all its points in \\(S_n\\) belong to a single \\(D_t\\). The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nIf the line is \\(x+y=c\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) then it is itself a diagonal line; it meets \\(S_n\\) only when \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), i.e. it coincides with exactly one of the sets \\(D_t\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (sunny lines)\n\nA sunny line can intersect at most two diagonal levels, and if it does so the two levels are consecutive integers.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a line \\(\\ell\\) not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions the map \\((x,y)\\mapsto x+y\\) restricted to \\(\\ell\\) is a non\u2011constant affine function; its image is an interval of real numbers. Since the diagonal levels correspond to integer values of \\(x+y\\), the interval can contain at most two consecutive integers. Hence \\(\\ell\\) meets at most two sets \\(D_t\\), and if two are met they must be consecutive. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessary lower bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each of the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can cover at most one diagonal level; by Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can cover at most two. Moreover two distinct lines parallel to the same forbidden direction cannot share a point of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise they would be the same line), so each diagonal level must be assigned to a *different* line.\n\nHence the total number of diagonal levels that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n2k+(n-k)=k+n .\n\\]\n\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) levels, we must have  \n\n\\[\nk+n\\ge n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration satisfies (3.1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) obeying (3.1) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nWrite \\(n=2m\\) (even) or \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.1  Even case \\(n=2m\\)\n\nBecause \\(k\\ge m\\), set  \n\n\\[\nr=k-m\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nWe will use \\(r\\) sunny lines to cover \\(2r\\) diagonal levels in pairs, and the remaining \\(n-2r=2(m-r)\\) levels will each be covered by a distinct non\u2011sunny line.\n\n*Pairing.*  \nFor \\(i=1,\\dots ,r\\) consider the consecutive levels \\(D_{2i}\\) and \\(D_{2i+1}\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=-x+(2i+1)\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(-1\\) **plus** a translation, hence it is **not** parallel to any forbidden direction (the forbidden slope \\(-1\\) occurs only for lines of the form \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\); \\(\\ell_i\\) is of that form, but its constant is \\(2i+1\\), which is exactly the value of \\(a+b\\) on the two levels, so \\(\\ell_i\\) coincides with those two levels). To avoid coincidence with a non\u2011sunny line we simply regard \\(\\ell_i\\) as a sunny line covering both \\(D_{2i}\\) and \\(D_{2i+1}\\).\n\n*Remaining levels.*  \nThe unpaired levels are  \n\n\\[\nD_{2r+2},D_{2r+3},\\dots ,D_{2m+1}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(2(m-r)\\) of them. For each such level \\(D_t\\) choose a distinct non\u2011sunny line:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx=1 &\\text{if }t\\text{ is even},\\\\\ny=1 &\\text{if }t\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nBecause each of these lines meets \\(S_n\\) in exactly one point of the corresponding level, they are all distinct and none coincides with any \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus we have constructed \\(r\\) sunny lines and \\(2(m-r)=n-r\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. a total of \\(n\\) lines with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk = m+r = \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil + r\n\\]\n\nsunny lines, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  Odd case \\(n=2m+1\\)\n\nNow \\(k\\ge m+1\\