Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(a\\le n\\).  \nSetting \\(i=a\\) we compute\n\n\\[\nm_a a + c_a\n   =\\frac{a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\\,a + \\frac{n+1-2a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\n   =\\frac{a^2 + n+1-2a}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\n   =\\frac{a(a-2)+n+1}{\\,n+1-a\\,}\n   =b,\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows from the relation \\(a+b=n+1-(n+1-a-b)=b\\).  \nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_a\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_a\\).\n\nTherefore the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Mixed families for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* **Non\u2011sunny part.**  \n  Take the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n  (If \\(k=0\\) this collection is empty.)  \n  These lines are non\u2011sunny and cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(k\\).\n\n* **Sunny part.**  \n  For each \\(i\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) use the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) defined in \u00a73.\n\nNow consider any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a>k\\) then \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and, as shown in \u00a73, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_a\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen \\(n\\) lines.  \nExactly \\(k\\) of the lines are the vertical (non\u2011sunny) ones, and the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are sunny.  \nThus we have constructed a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines