\) and \\(-1\\), so every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(n\\) lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Since \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nb-a \\ge 1-a \\ge -(n-1),\\qquad\nb-a \\le (n+1-a)-a = n+1-2a \\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge 1\\), the lower bound improves to \\(b-a\\ge 0\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n0\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nSet \\(d:=b-a\\).  Then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b = a + d\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_d\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines, and we have a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Mixed configurations for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\n(if \\(k=0\\) this set is empty).  These lines are non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nTake the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\; y = x + d\\qquad\\text{for }d=k,k+1,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-k\\) such lines, all distinct and sunny.\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a>k\\) then \\(a\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  As shown in §3, the offset \\(d:=b-a\\) satisfies \\(k\\le d\\le n-1\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_d\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the chosen \\(n\\) lines.  Exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are vertical (non‑sunny) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) are sunny, so the configuration contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\l