  Since \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n-(n-1)\\le b-a\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{b-a}\\) and belongs to the family.  \nConsequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\), giving a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Mixed configurations for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nV_{j}:\\; x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\n(If \\(k=n\\) this set is empty.)  These lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(-(k-1)\\le i\\le k-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_{i}:\\; y = x + i .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(S_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are therefore sunny.  There are exactly \\(k\\) of them, and they are distinct because their intercepts differ.\n\nWe now prove that every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(|b-a|\\le k-1\\).*  \nThen \\(i:=b-a\\) belongs to \\(\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_{i}\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(|b-a|\\ge k\\).*  \nAssume \\(b-a\\ge k\\) (the opposite inequality is handled symmetrically).  \nFrom \\(b-a\\ge k\\) and the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a+k)=n+1-a-k,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \nThus the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (present because \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).  \nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the lines \\(S_{i}\\)); the remaining \\(n-k\\) are vertical and therefore non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have ex